---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security"
  description="What mdbase stores and can read for Cloud copy and Private collections, how devices, apps and members get access, the known limits, and how to report a security issue."
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <h1>Security</h1>
    <p class="page-intro__lede">
      What mdbase stores and can read for each kind of collection, who holds
      the keys, how access is granted and removed, and the limits that apply.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="every-collection">
    <h2 id="every-collection">In every synced collection</h2>
    <p>
      Each change is encrypted and signed on the device that made it before it
      leaves. Changes are encrypted with the collection key using
      ChaCha20-Poly1305 and signed with the device’s Ed25519 key. mdbase stores
      the encrypted changes, snapshots and attachments, puts changes in order,
      and passes them to the other devices. The service that stores them never
      holds the collection key, and every device checks each change’s signature
      and permissions for itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apps connect to a copy of the collection over an encrypted session (Noise)
      that ends at that copy. When the session passes through mdbase’s relay,
      the relay forwards encrypted traffic and sees who connects to which
      device, along with sizes and timing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="by-option">
    <h2 id="by-option">Cloud copy and Private</h2>
    <table class="trust-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Question</th>
          <th>Cloud copy</th>
          <th>Private</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Who holds the collection key</td>
          <td>
            Your devices, plus mdbase’s hosted service and key escrow. Their keys
            are wrapped by a key management service and unwrapped only in memory.
          </td>
          <td>Only your devices, and your account key if you use one (below)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Record contents, paths, field names and attachments</td>
          <td>Readable by mdbase’s hosted service, which serves apps from its copy</td>
          <td>Never readable by mdbase</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Where apps connect</td>
          <td>mdbase’s hosted copy</td>
          <td>One of your devices, through the relay</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A new app permission takes effect</td>
          <td>When you approve it in Connect</td>
          <td>When you approve it in Connect and one of your devices confirms it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A new device of yours gets the key</td>
          <td>After you sign in; mdbase delivers the key once your account approves the device</td>
          <td>After you enter your encryption password or recovery key, or approve it from another device in strict mode</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      In both, mdbase can see your account, a collection’s members and their
      roles, its devices, app permissions, the number and padded sizes of
      changes and attachments, when they were made, and connection details such
      as IP addresses. If an app sets reminders, mdbase stores each reminder’s
      time and a random ID. For a Private collection, it stores no reminder
      content.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="private-keys">
    <h2 id="private-keys">Private collection keys</h2>
    <p>
      By default, your devices share an account key. Your device generates it
      and shows it to you once as a recovery key, and mdbase stores a copy
      locked with your encryption password using Argon2id. mdbase never sees
      the password or the recovery key. A new device that you sign in gets the
      collection keys once you enter either one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Strict mode removes that stored copy. Each new device is then approved
      from a device you already have, by checking that both show the same
      six-digit code. The code mixes a random value from each device, and the
      new device commits to its value first, so a device substituted by someone
      else would match only by chance, about one in a million per attempt. An
      owner or editor approves a new member’s first device the same way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="removing-access">
    <h2 id="removing-access">Removing access</h2>
    <p>
      Removing a device or member changes the collection key. Changes made after
      that are encrypted with a key the removed device never receives. A removed
      device or member keeps whatever it already had, including its copy of the
      files.
    </p>
    <p>
      Revoking an app permission ends its sessions. Every copy of the collection
      stops accepting the app’s requests once it sees the revocation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="sync-off">
    <h2 id="sync-off">Collections with sync off</h2>
    <p>
      Collections moved from the Connector start with sync off, and any
      collection can be set to sync off in its advanced settings. mdbase stores
      none of their contents. Apps reach them through mdbase’s relay to the
      computer that holds them, while it’s running.
    </p>
    <p>
      For these collections, the background service on your computer trusts the
      app permissions that mdbase issues. It notifies you when a new app gains
      access and keeps a list of apps you can review and revoke. To require your
      approval on the computer as well, turn on{" "}
      <code>mdbase settings --require-approval on</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        mdbase can’t read a Private collection, but it does deliver its changes.
        Devices assume that mdbase delivers changes in order and hides none. A
        compromised service that withheld a device removal could keep that
        device receiving new changes. Devices don’t yet detect this.
      </li>
      <li>
        When a collection switches from Cloud copy to Private, content stored
        before the switch remains readable with the keys mdbase held until it is
        re-encrypted. You’re offered a step that re-encrypts it.
      </li>
      <li>
        The web apps that mdbase publishes see what they show you. Private
        protects what mdbase stores and relays, and relies on the app you run
        being the one you expect.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the copy of your account key that mdbase stores were stolen, a weak
        encryption password could be guessed offline. Choose a strong one, or use
        strict mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        Obsidian plugins run in the same process as the mdbase plugin and have
        full access to the vault and its collections.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="report">
    <h2 id="report">Report a security issue</h2>
    <p>
      Use GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting on the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/mdbase-dev/mdbase-connect/security">mdbase-connect repository</a>.
      Don’t open a public issue. Include the affected component and version, the
      steps to reproduce, and the impact, with credentials, paths and record
      contents removed. Developers building apps should also read the{" "}
      <a href="/sdk/security/">SDK security guidance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
